    "created_at": "2016-02-29T04:16:40Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-29T04:16:40Z",
    "body": "> Well for one this statement is wrong since all changes to attributes have an effect. They queue mutation records.\n\nThe statement doesn't say anything about changes having or not having an effect. It says that the term \"change\", when used in this specification in the context of content attributes, refers to only cases where the attribute has changed per the DOM definition, _and the value is different than the previous one_. It doesn't talk about effects.\n\n---\n\nHere are some examples of the type of analysis I would really like to have:\n\n> The base element is the first base element in tree order with an href content attribute in its Document, and its href content attribute is changed.\n\nThis will be OK after the change, since re-setting the frozen base URL will not give a different result if the value of the href attribute is not different. (I think.)\n\n> When the href attribute of the link element of an external resource link that is already in a Document is changed.\n\nThis is a potentially serious difference. Per the current spec, link resources should not be re-fetched if their href attribute is set to the same value as it currently is. With your change, it would be normatively required to re-fetch. What do browsers do?\n\n(There are similar clauses for link's type and crossorigin attributes.)\n\n> The element's src, srcset, width, or sizes attributes are set, changed, or removed.\n\nThis has similar re-fetching implications for images, although it seems like maybe it short-circuits if the image is already loaded. But, it will definitely cause animations to reset. (Presumably impacting animated gifs.)\n\n(There is a similar clause for source elements inside picture.)\n\n> Whenever an iframe element with a nested browsing context has its srcdoc attribute set, changed, or removed, the user agent must process the iframe attributes.\n\nThis will cause `<iframe srcdoc>`s to re-navigate when doing `iframe.srcdoc = iframe.srcdoc`. What do browsers do?\n\n---\n\nThat is me doing a Ctrl+F for \"change\" in the spec and getting about 1/5 of the way down the page. Already these are pretty huge normative changes.\n\nIf you are really concerned about the terminology mismatch between how HTML and DOM use \"change\", then you could consider replacing HTML's term with \"mutate\" or some such. It will be a big undertaking to find all the uses (maybe there is a better strategy than Ctrl+Fing for \"change\", but I am not sure what it is). But on the other hand getting them xrefed would be useful.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
